WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.880
When people envision a future in art
most often it's seen through a white lens.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:12.090
Only 8% of the 100 top grossing sci-fi and fantasy films featured a protagonist of color.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.640
Half the time the protagonist was Will Smith.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.140
The future looks different, if you look at it through the lens of black experiences.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.320
But we’re not all white or Will Smith.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.840
The future looks different, if you look at it through the lens of black experiences.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.140
That’s why we have this thing called Afrofuturism.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:32.960
The term was coined by culture critic Mark Dery in 1994 to point out the lack of black writers and black stories in science fiction.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.180
But this kind of art existed long before the term Afrofuturism did. Just look at music.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.940
Jazz musician Sun-Ra is probably one of the most recognizable Afrofuturists.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:51.440
He lays out an afrofuturistic design is his 1973 album Space is the Place, which you’re listening to now, and also became a film.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:58.000
The key is that his future was afrocentric – linking his futuristic self, to ancient forbears in Egypt.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.260
These ideas were more politically direct, and a lot more funky, in George Clinton’s Parliament during 1975.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.300
In “Mothership Connection” he connects the struggle of civil rights and “We Shall Overcome” to a positive future with evidence the struggle will work:

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.860
"You have overcome. For I am here"

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.140
He not only references an old song from the underground railroad to escape slavery:

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.940
"Swing down, sweet chariot.
Stop and let me ride."

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.800
But he flipped a desperate history into positive futuristic ride on his spaceship as evidence the struggle is over and we’ve won

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.500
"Swing down, sweet chariot. Stop and let me ride"

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.660
— so of course everybody wants to get on.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.920
[Dr. Dre's "Let Me Ride" playing]

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.980
And people did get on,

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.240
[Dr. Dre's "Let Me Ride" continues]

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.480
or at least some 90s hip hop heavyweights did.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:54.240
But Afrofuturism also critiques the way the future looks today like André 3000 did on his ATLiens verse.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.660
“Because the future of the world depends on"

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.900
"If or if not the child we raise gon' have that n***a syndrome."

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.280
"Or will it know to beat the odds regardless of the skin tone.”

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.660
He wants his unborn kid to get the future they want, not the one society created for them because of race.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.360
And that’s why he takes pride in the difference.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.240
They alienate us cause we different keep your hands to the sky.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.200
Like Sounds of Blackness when I practice what I preach ain't no lie.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.120
And empowers black people to fight to get what’s ours.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.440
"I'll be the baker and the maker of the piece of my pie."

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:29.260
'Cause that’s the thing about afrofuturism — it’s rooted in black people a better future for ourselves on our terms, like Janelle Monáe does in "Q.U.E.E.N."

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.880
"Even if it makes others uncomfortable"

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.080
"I will love who I am."

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.200
This is the common thread of Afrofuturism. To overcome the current ways society remains unequal

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.720
There needs to be futures where those problems are solved.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.380
Afrofuturism shows us what that looks like.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.560
It looks like hope.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.820
[Crowd shouting "We goin' be alright."

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.900
"Will you be electric sheep? Electric ladies, will you sleep? Or will you preach?"

